<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis II, Count of Flanders</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1330–1384</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Louis of Male</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis II, known as Louis of Male, was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> from 1346 to 1384 and also count of Nevers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rethel">Rethel</a>. He steered between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and Mechelen from Brabant, and faced a long revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, which French help defeated at Roosebeke in 1382. The marriage of his heiress Margaret to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, brought Flanders into the Burgundian state on his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-ii-count-of-flanders.jpg" alt="Louis II, Count of Flanders" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis de Male (1330-1384 ; comte de Flandre). Autorité émettrice de monnaie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Pays-Bas,_Flandre,_Louis_II_de_Male,_royal_-_btv1b11341332k_(1_of_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
